{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "04-beta.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "β-reduction and normal forms",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Reduce terms to normal form, meet terms that have none, and see why the order of the steps can decide whether you finish but never where.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Keep taking β-steps, and three questions come up at once. Does it stop? If it stops, is the end the same whichever redex you pick each time? And can the choice decide whether it stops at all?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Where it stops",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Normal form\n> A term with no redex is in **normal form**: it cannot take a step, and it is the term's answer. A term **has** a normal form when some sequence of β-steps reaches one.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "(λx. λy. y x) a (λz. z)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null,
      "stepwise": 0
    },
    {
      "src": "`S`, `K` and `I` are library combinators, unfolded in one δ-step: $S = \\lambda x\\ y\\ z.\\, x\\ z\\ (y\\ z)$, $K = \\lambda x\\ y.\\, x$, $I = \\lambda x.\\, x$. $S\\ K\\ K$ turns out to be the identity:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "S K K a",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Terms that never stop",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A β-step uses up a λ, but it also copies the argument once for each use of the parameter. So look for a term that, applied to itself, makes a copy of itself.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> $\\omega = \\lambda x.\\, x\\ x$ applies its argument to itself. What is $\\omega\\ \\omega$ after one step?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The library calls $\\omega$ `omega`. A step count after the strategy's name shows that many steps instead of refusing:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "normal 2: omega omega",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$\\Omega = \\omega\\ \\omega$ steps to itself, for ever. The engine gives up:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "omega omega",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Triple instead of double, and the term grows as it goes, until the engine stops it:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "(λx. x x x) (λx. x x x)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Does the order matter?",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$(\\lambda x.\\, \\lambda y.\\, y)\\ \\Omega$ has two redexes: the whole term, and $\\Omega$ inside it. Reduce $\\Omega$ and you are back where you started, for ever. Reduce the outer one and $\\Omega$ is thrown away, since $x$ is not used:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "normal: (λx. λy. y) (omega omega)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "So the order can decide **whether** you reach a normal form. Can it decide **which**? Take a term where both paths finish: $(\\lambda x.\\, x\\ x)\\ ((\\lambda y.\\, y)\\ z)$. Outer redex first copies the inner redex and reduces it twice; inner first (`applicative:`, lesson 5) reduces it once and copies the result.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Predict both answers, and which path is shorter.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "normal: (λx. x x) ((λy. y) z)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "applicative: (λx. x x) ((λy. y) z)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Three steps one way, two the other, and the same end. That is no accident:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] Church–Rosser\n> If $M$ reduces to $N_1$ and to $N_2$ (each in any number of steps), then $N_1$ and $N_2$ both reduce to some common $P$. So a term has **at most one** normal form, up to α: the order of the steps can change whether you get there, never where.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "That is why a λ-term has a meaning, and why an exercise can compare normal forms.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "η",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$\\lambda x.\\, f\\ x$ and $f$ are different terms, both in normal form. Yet applied to any $a$, both give $f\\ a$. Should they count as equal?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] η-reduction\n> $\\lambda x.\\, f\\ x$ reduces to $f$ when $x$ is not free in $f$. Adding this rule says that a function is determined by what it does (**extensionality**).",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "eta: λx. λy. f x y",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "eta: λx. x x",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$\\lambda x.\\, x\\ x$ is not an η-redex: $x$ is free in the function part, so dropping the λ would change what $x$ means.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In Lean",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A step contracts the leftmost-outermost redex; evaluation takes steps on fuel, because some terms never stop.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "/-- One normal-order step: contract the leftmost-outermost redex, if there is one. -/\ndef betaStep : Term → Option Term\n  | app (lam x b) a => some (subst x a b)\n  | app f a =>\n    match betaStep f with\n    | some f' => some (app f' a)\n    | none => (betaStep a).map (app f ·)\n  | lam x b => (betaStep b).map (lam x ·)\n  | var _ => none\n\n/-- Take up to fuel steps. -/\ndef eval : Nat → Term → Term\n  | 0, t => t\n  | n + 1, t =>\n    match betaStep t with\n    | none => t\n    | some t' => eval n t'\n\n#eval (eval 10 (app (app K (var \"a\")) (var \"b\"))).pretty\n#eval (eval 3 (app ω ω)).pretty",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem eval_normal (t : Term) (h : betaStep t = none) : ∀ n, eval n t = t := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "A normal form stays put: evaluating it, with any fuel, gives it back.",
      "leanSolution": "  intro n\n  cases n <;> simp [eval, h]",
      "hints": [
        "Introduce `n` and split `cases n`: no fuel, or some.",
        "`simp [eval, h]` settles both."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "(λx. λy. x) y",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce $(\\lambda x.\\, \\lambda y.\\, x)\\ y$ to normal form. (The trap: $\\lambda y.\\, y$ is wrong.)",
      "hints": [
        "Rename the inner binder first, say to $z$: $\\lambda z.\\, y$."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "(λf. λx. f (f x)) (λy. y y)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce $(\\lambda f.\\, \\lambda x.\\, f\\ (f\\ x))\\ (\\lambda y.\\, y\\ y)$ to normal form.",
      "hints": [
        "After the first step: $\\lambda x.\\, (\\lambda y.\\, y\\ y)\\ ((\\lambda y.\\, y\\ y)\\ x)$. Normal order then reduces the outer redex."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "(λx. λy. x) (λz. z) (omega omega)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "$(\\lambda x.\\, \\lambda y.\\, x)\\ (\\lambda z.\\, z)\\ \\Omega$ has a normal form. Choose your redexes well and find it.",
      "hints": [
        "Never touch $\\Omega$: the function throws its second argument away."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "eta: λx. (λy. g y) x",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce $\\lambda x.\\, (\\lambda y.\\, g\\ y)\\ x$ with β and η.",
      "hints": [
        "$\\lambda y.\\, g\\ y$ is an η-redex."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> A normal form is a term with no redex. Some terms have none, and some reach one only along the right path. By Church–Rosser a term has at most one normal form, so the order of steps decides whether you get there, never where. η adds extensionality: $\\lambda x.\\, f\\ x$ is $f$.\n\nIf the order can decide whether we finish, which order should an interpreter use? Lesson 5.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
